<p class="text-lg text-silver-400 leading-relaxed mb-6">
  Most bad used-car purchases fail in a predictable order. The buyer agrees on a payment before confirming the car is the car in the ad, skips the recall check because the seller said it was “up to date,” and discovers a branded title or a mismatched VIN after the money has moved. This hub is the sequence we would actually use, with the longer checklists linked where a single page would turn into a wall of identical advice.
</p>

<h2>Confirm the vehicle before you talk about price</h2>
<p>
  The 17-character VIN has been the identifier on cars built for the U.S. market since the 1981 model year. It is on the driver’s side of the dash, readable through the windshield, and on the driver’s door jamb. It should also match the title, the registration, and any window label. If those four do not match, you do not have a negotiation. You have a reason to leave. How the characters are organized is covered in <a href="/guides/how-to-decode-any-vin/">how to decode any VIN</a> and in the shorter <a href="/learn/what-is-a-vin/">what a VIN is</a> primer. The live lookup is the <a href="/vin-decoder/">VIN decoder</a>, which asks NHTSA’s product catalog for the year, make, model, plant, and the equipment the manufacturer reported.
</p>
<p>
  Read the decode against the ad line by line. A seller who lists a trim, an engine, or a drivetrain the VIN does not return is either guessing or misrepresenting the car. Neither one is a small thing once you are financing it. VIN cloning, in which a legitimate number is copied onto a stolen or rebuilt car, is described in <a href="/guides/how-vin-fraud-works/">how VIN fraud works</a>. The practical tell is paperwork and physical stamps that do not agree, not a clever line in the listing.
</p>

<h2>Recalls, history, and title are three different files</h2>
<p>
  An open safety recall is a free dealer repair under 49 U.S.C. Chapter 301. It is not a reason to walk away by itself, and a seller’s assurance that “there are no recalls” is not a check. Run the VIN through the <a href="/recall-checker/">recall checker</a> or through <a href="https://www.nhtsa.gov/recalls" rel="noopener">NHTSA’s own search</a>. Ask for the dealer invoice if the seller says a campaign is already done. What an open campaign does to a later resale is discussed in <a href="/guides/recalls-resale-value/">recalls and resale value</a>.
</p>
<p>
  A free VIN decode does not contain accident history, odometer readings, or title brands. Those come from state title records and from insurers, which is why a paid history report and a free decode answer different questions. The comparison is in <a href="/guides/carfax-vs-vin-lookup/">Carfax versus a free VIN lookup</a> and in the broader <a href="/guides/car-history-report-comparison/">history-report comparison</a>. Use the free data first so you know which car you are buying a report on.
</p>
<p>
  Title brands are state records. A salvage, flood, or rebuilt brand in one state is not automatically printed the same way after the car moves. That gap is why title washing works at all. Start with the <a href="/guides/title/salvage-title-guide/">salvage-title guide</a>, the <a href="/guides/hidden-salvage-title-warning-signs/">warning signs</a>, and the <a href="/guides/state-title-rules-comparison/">state title-rule comparison</a> if the car has lived in more than one state. The Federal Trade Commission’s used-car guidance for dealer sales is the booklet <a href="https://www.bulkorder.ftc.gov/publications/buying-used-car-dealer" rel="noopener">Buying a Used Car From a Dealer</a>. A dealer’s Buyers Guide is a disclosure document. Read it. Private-party sales do not come with that form, which is a reason to be slower, not a reason to skip the VIN.
</p>

<h2>Inspect the car, then the contract</h2>
<p>
  Once the identity checks out, look at the car. The short list is the <a href="/guides/buying/used-car-checklist/">25-point used-car checklist</a>. The longer version, including how to read a test drive and when to pay an independent shop, is <a href="/guides/how-to-check-car-before-buying/">how to check a car before buying</a>. An inspection is worth more on a car old enough that the factory warranty is gone, and on any car whose history report has a gap you cannot explain. The shop should be one you chose, not one the seller named as “their guy.”
</p>
<p>
  Safety design is a separate pass from the inspection. A clean mechanical check does not make a poorly rated body style into a good one. Pull NHTSA and IIHS results for that model year before you fall in love with a price. The <a href="/guides/safety/">safety hub</a> explains how those two systems differ.
</p>
<p>
  Financing is part of the purchase, not a favor the desk does after you have said yes. Get a pre-qualification from a bank or credit union before you arrive, so a dealer’s rate is something you can compare. The <a href="/guides/financing/">financing hub</a> and the <a href="/guides/financing/auto-loan-rates/">auto-loan rates guide</a> cover the terms that change the total you pay: APR, term length, and what is included in the amount financed. The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau’s auto-loan tools are at <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/consumer-tools/auto-loans/" rel="noopener">consumerfinance.gov</a>.
</p>
<p>
  Add-on products sold in the finance office — service contracts, gap waivers, etch, paint protection — are optional. A service contract is not the manufacturer’s warranty. Read the exclusions, the deductible, and who is actually obligated to pay the shop. That market is covered in the <a href="/guides/warranties/">warranty hub</a>. The FTC’s explanation of auto warranties and service contracts is in <a href="https://consumer.ftc.gov/consumer-alerts/2023/03/what-know-about-auto-service-contracts-extended-warranty-scams" rel="noopener">what to know about auto service contracts and extended warranty scams</a>.
</p>

<h2>Price is a comparison, not a slogan</h2>
<p>
  A trade-in credit, a private-party price, and an instant offer from a company that buys cars are three different transactions. Dealers deduct reconditioning and their own margin from a trade. A buyer who pays you directly does not. The FTC’s current page on a dealer trade-in, <a href="https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/auto-trade-ins-and-negative-equity-when-you-owe-more-your-car-worth" rel="noopener">Auto Trade-Ins and Negative Equity</a>, says to compare the offers in front of you and notes that selling the car yourself may bring more than the dealer’s figure. We do not print a national percentage for how far apart those offers “usually” are, because that number is not a fact you can take to a desk. How to use a competing number in the conversation is in <a href="/blog/how-to-negotiate-used-car-price/">how to negotiate a used-car price</a>.
</p>
<p>
  If you are trading a car in, get the purchase price of the new car settled before you introduce the trade. Blending the two lets a strong price on one side hide a weak number on the other. And if the car you are trading has an open recall, say so and show the VIN printout. Hiding it does not survive the buyer’s own check.
</p>
<p>
  New-car buying is simpler on identity and harder on the option sheet. The window sticker (the Monroney label) is the manufacturer’s list of equipment and price. Dealer-added accessories are a separate piece of paper. Treat them as negotiable, because they are. Incentives change monthly and are often tied to captive finance. Read the contract to see whether a rebate required you to take the dealer’s loan, and what it costs to refinance later.
</p>

<h2>A short sequence</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Decode the VIN and match it to the title and the ad.</li>
  <li>Check open recalls, and ask for proof of any repair the seller claims is done.</li>
  <li>Read the title brand and, if the price is serious, a history report.</li>
  <li>Inspect the car, or pay a shop that does not work for the seller.</li>
  <li>Compare financing and any service contract as separate decisions from the car’s price.</li>
  <li>Compare a trade-in with at least one other offer before you sign.</li>
</ol>

